    {
      "id": "sec-002-background",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Background",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "labelStart": "2",
      "labelEnd": "6",
      "words": 1617,
      "summary": {
        "text": "Explains the background to WHO digital adaptation kits, noting that despite investment in digital health, the data and logic in digital tools are often opaque, inconsistent or proprietary and not clearly linked to evidence-based recommendations. It describes the kits as software-neutral, standardised documentation that distils WHO guidance into content for digital tracking and decision-support systems, mapped to standard terminologies such as ICD. It defines digital tracking and decision support and identifies the core WHO family planning guidance documents the kit draws on, such as the medical eligibility criteria for contraceptive use.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "pocs patch",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "amenorrhoea patch",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "surgical sterilization",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "sterilization ring",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "coitus interruptus",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "sterilization intrauterine",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "patch female",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "ring ecps",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "Digital health – defined broadly as the systematic application of information and communications technologies (ICT), computer science and data to support informed decision-making by individuals, the health workforce and health systems, to strengthen resilience to disease and improve health and wellness (1) – is increasingly being applied as an essential enabler for health service delivery and accountability.",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-003-objectives",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Objectives",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "labelStart": "6",
      "labelEnd": "7",
      "words": 347,
      "summary": {
        "text": "States the objectives of the family planning digital adaptation kit: to keep digital tracking and decision-support systems consistent with WHO guidelines, give health and digital teams a shared understanding of content, and supply core data elements and decision logic. It lists the source guidelines, notes that outputs are generic and must be adapted locally, and mentions related kits for antenatal care, HIV and STIs.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "software developers",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "dak",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "health content",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "self-care interventions",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "digital systems",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "health programme",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "decision-support",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "daks",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "This DAK focuses on family planning and aims to provide a common language across various audiences – family planning and other programme managers, software developers, and implementers of digital systems – to ensure a common understanding of the appropriate health information content within a defined health programme area, as a mechanism to catalyse the effective use of these digital systems. The key objectives of",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-004-components-of-a-digital-adaptation-kit",
      "number": null,
      "title": "Components of a digital adaptation kit",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "labelStart": "7",
      "labelEnd": "11",
      "words": 1275,
      "summary": {
        "text": "Describes the eight interlinked components of a digital adaptation kit, from health interventions and recommendations, personas and user scenarios through workflows, core data elements, decision-support logic, indicators and system requirements. A table sets out each component's purpose, outputs and adaptation needs. It also explains the identifier conventions used for data elements, decision tables, indicators and requirements.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
      "keywords": [
        {
          "term": "adaptation kit",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "digital adaptation",
          "heading": true
        },
        {
          "term": "decision-support logic",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "non-functional requirement",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "decision-support table",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "functional requirement",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "generic personas",
          "heading": false
        },
        {
          "term": "existing workflows",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "The DAK comprises eight interlinked components: (1) health interventions and associated recommendations; (2) generic personas; (3) user scenarios; (4) business processes and workflows; (5) core data elements; (6) decision-support logic; (7) indicators and performance metrics; and (8) high-level functional and non-functional requirements. Table 1 provides an overview of each of the contributing components of the DAK,",
      "extractCut": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sec-005-how-to-use-this-digital-adaptation-kit",
      "number": null,
      "title": "How to use this digital adaptation kit",
      "level": 1,
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "labelStart": "11",
      "labelEnd": "13",
      "words": 841,
      "summary": {
        "text": "Identifies ministry of health family planning programme managers as the main audience for the kit, working with digital health and information system counterparts and business analysts. It explains how the kit supports collaboration and adaptation to local contexts and notes that client-facing digital tools will be covered in a forthcoming self-care kit.",
        "status": "draft"
      },
